  ],
  "text": "Your voice is riding on a wave you cannot see, and the receiver strips that wave away to hand it back. A voice on its own does not travel. Its wavelengths are far too clumsy for an aerial. So you make a fast, steady wave that does travel, and you ride the voice on top of it. Amplitude modulation makes the carrier louder and quieter in the shape of the voice. Frequency modulation keeps it the same size and squeezes and stretches it instead. And each band behaves differently, which is why the dial matters more than it seems. Long waves bend around hills. Short ones need a clear line of sight. And at night, the upper atmosphere changes and bounces long waves back down, so they carry much further. Which means the spectrum is finite, divided, and worth an enormous amount of money. Nobody owns light. Everybody owns a slice of it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
